1
00:01:12,773 --> 00:01:14,071
So you knew full well

2
00:01:14,241 --> 00:01:17,609
when this issue of the
<i>National Informer</i> went to press

3
00:01:17,778 --> 00:01:21,180
that this photo of my client would
be featured on the front page?

4
00:01:21,348 --> 00:01:24,341
Of course, I approve of
everything that goes to press.

5
00:01:25,452 --> 00:01:28,581
- Did you bother to verify it?
- Yes.

6
00:01:28,755 --> 00:01:31,452
Our staff talked to the
doorman at the hotel

7
00:01:31,625 --> 00:01:34,925
who confirmed that Reverend
Masters was indeed there

8
00:01:35,095 --> 00:01:37,792
on the day in
question with a woman.

9
00:01:38,365 --> 00:01:41,824
We have just heard my
client testify, under oath,

10
00:01:42,002 --> 00:01:44,665
that he was not with
a woman on that day.

11
00:01:44,838 --> 00:01:46,363
How do you explain
that, Mr. Buckley?

12
00:01:46,540 --> 00:01:49,237
Well, the hotel records
show that he had two entrees

13
00:01:49,409 --> 00:01:51,037
and two drinks
for lunch that day.

14
00:01:51,211 --> 00:01:53,043
And how does he explain that?

15
00:01:53,213 --> 00:01:56,274
He was hungry
and he was thirsty.

16
00:01:56,450 --> 00:01:58,316
Now answer my question, please.

17
00:01:58,485 --> 00:02:01,785
He's a married man and his
TV ministry is worth millions.

18
00:02:01,955 --> 00:02:04,322
Why, he's not about to
admit he's an adulterer.

19
00:02:04,691 --> 00:02:05,886
That, sir, is a lie!

20
00:02:06,059 --> 00:02:09,393
And you know that it's a lie, and
God Almighty knows that it's a lie.

21
00:02:09,563 --> 00:02:13,398
And I know for a fact that you will
burn in hell for this for all eternity.

22
00:02:13,567 --> 00:02:16,127
- Seeing is believing, reverend.
- That's enough.

23
00:02:16,303 --> 00:02:18,135
The plaintiff is
instructed to sit down

24
00:02:18,305 --> 00:02:21,833
and warned to refrain
from any further outbursts.

25
00:02:25,178 --> 00:02:28,615
Actually, Mr. Buckley,
seeing is not believing.

26
00:02:29,082 --> 00:02:30,448
Not anymore.

27
00:02:30,617 --> 00:02:33,143
Not when
computer-generated dinosaurs

28
00:02:33,320 --> 00:02:36,722
can interact on-screen
with flesh-and-blood actors,

29
00:02:36,890 --> 00:02:39,826
when objects can be added
to or deleted from photographs

30
00:02:39,993 --> 00:02:42,189
with the greatest
of digitalized ease.

31
00:02:42,362 --> 00:02:45,230
No, nowadays, photos
are all too easily faked.

32
00:02:45,399 --> 00:02:48,631
- Aren't they?
- That photo is not a fake.

33
00:02:48,802 --> 00:02:50,134
Then where'd it come from?

34
00:02:50,737 --> 00:02:52,865
That is privileged information.

35
00:02:53,040 --> 00:02:56,772
But it is someone who,
like your staff photographers,

36
00:02:56,943 --> 00:03:01,881
spends his days lurking in the
bushes outside the homes of celebrities

37
00:03:02,049 --> 00:03:04,541
or sneaking into
popular watering holes

38
00:03:04,718 --> 00:03:07,210
just so he can catch famous
people on film doing something,

39
00:03:07,387 --> 00:03:10,380
anything that might be construed
as titillating, right, Mr. Buckley?

40
00:03:10,557 --> 00:03:12,685
Even as we speak.

41
00:03:18,398 --> 00:03:19,832
Yes.

42
00:03:22,235 --> 00:03:26,036
- This is perfect.
- Yes, this'll be good for you here.

43
00:03:26,206 --> 00:03:28,402
- Thank you.
- Enjoy your meal.

44
00:03:28,575 --> 00:03:30,737
Ms. Carol Davis.

45
00:03:31,978 --> 00:03:34,812
- Thank you.
- It's brilliant.

46
00:03:36,683 --> 00:03:38,675
I guess it's time for
me to toast all of you.

47
00:03:41,855 --> 00:03:43,153
Oh, are you all right?

48
00:03:45,025 --> 00:03:46,550
- Help her up.
- Here we are.

49
00:03:46,727 --> 00:03:49,026
Oh, are you okay?

50
00:03:50,497 --> 00:03:51,692
Hey, hey!

51
00:03:52,099 --> 00:03:53,658
Who are you? Stop it.

52
00:03:54,568 --> 00:03:56,730
- Make him stop it.
- Hey, what's going on here?

53
00:03:56,903 --> 00:03:58,394
Hey, somebody stop this guy.

54
00:04:00,273 --> 00:04:01,741
- Stop that man.
MAîTRE D': Sir...

55
00:04:04,177 --> 00:04:06,112
Since the defendant
is unwilling to name

56
00:04:06,279 --> 00:04:09,738
or locate the person who
allegedly took this picture,

57
00:04:09,916 --> 00:04:13,114
the testimony of only one
other person is relevant here:

58
00:04:13,286 --> 00:04:16,484
that of this mysterious woman.

59
00:04:16,990 --> 00:04:21,553
Now, it wasn't easy, but
we managed to locate her.

60
00:04:23,130 --> 00:04:25,827
Please call Ms. Heather
Hartley to the stand.

61
00:04:26,867 --> 00:04:28,995
The State calls Heather Hartley.

62
00:04:29,169 --> 00:04:31,638
Is this you in this
picture, Ms. Hartley?

63
00:04:31,805 --> 00:04:33,273
Yes, it is.

64
00:04:33,840 --> 00:04:36,537
So, you were with my
client, Wesley Masters,

65
00:04:36,710 --> 00:04:39,976
at the Crown Regent
Hotel on September 28th?

66
00:04:40,147 --> 00:04:41,479
No, I was not.

67
00:04:42,315 --> 00:04:44,443
- You weren't?
- I wasn't anywhere near

68
00:04:44,618 --> 00:04:46,052
the Crown Regent Hotel that day.

69
00:04:46,219 --> 00:04:48,245
I was up in Charleston
visiting friends.

70
00:04:48,421 --> 00:04:50,754
Well, maybe somebody
got their dates mixed up.

71
00:04:50,924 --> 00:04:53,223
Maybe this picture was
taken on some other day.

72
00:04:53,393 --> 00:04:55,726
- Impossible.
- Why do you say that?

73
00:04:55,896 --> 00:04:58,388
Because I've never so
much as met Wesley Masters,

74
00:04:58,565 --> 00:05:01,160
let alone had lunch with
him or drinks with him,

75
00:05:01,334 --> 00:05:03,064
or anything else with him.

76
00:05:03,870 --> 00:05:05,168
The God's honest truth.

77
00:05:05,505 --> 00:05:07,804
- Praise the Lord.
- All right, that's enough.

78
00:05:07,974 --> 00:05:09,704
Settle down.

79
00:05:09,876 --> 00:05:13,069
<i>I don't love nobody
Don't nobody love me</i>

80
00:05:13,246 --> 00:05:17,206
<i>Oh, yes, I'm looking for that
bully That bully of the town</i>

81
00:05:17,384 --> 00:05:20,786
<i>Lord, I'm looking for
that bully Of the town</i>

82
00:05:20,954 --> 00:05:24,823
<i>Oh, yes, I'm looking for that
bully That bully can't be found</i>

83
00:05:24,991 --> 00:05:28,928
<i>Lord, I'm looking for
that bully Of the town</i>

84
00:05:29,095 --> 00:05:32,725
<i>I don't love nobody
Don't nobody love me</i>

85
00:05:32,899 --> 00:05:35,592
<i>All they want is my money...</i> - Ben?
- What?

86
00:05:35,769 --> 00:05:36,896
- Ben?
- What?

87
00:05:37,070 --> 00:05:39,062
- Ben!
- What?

88
00:05:42,375 --> 00:05:44,571
I'm gonna sue
their lousy, sadistic,

89
00:05:44,744 --> 00:05:47,304
mendacious little hind
ends right into the ground.

90
00:05:47,480 --> 00:05:49,574
- Is that you?
- Yes, it's me.

91
00:05:49,749 --> 00:05:52,651
But they were implying that I
was drunk at the time, and I wasn't.

92
00:05:52,819 --> 00:05:54,082
You sure look drunk.

93
00:05:54,254 --> 00:05:57,383
I lost my footing
and lost my balance.

94
00:05:57,557 --> 00:05:59,822
Is that a wine
glass in your hand?

95
00:05:59,993 --> 00:06:01,655
I was about to make a toast.

96
00:06:01,828 --> 00:06:03,558
Oh, Carol Davis is
running for the Senate.

97
00:06:03,730 --> 00:06:07,531
This is Cliff Lewis, attorney at
law, Jerri Stone, investigator.

98
00:06:07,701 --> 00:06:08,930
Running for Senate?

99
00:06:09,102 --> 00:06:11,901
Well, you can't run for Senate if
everyone thinks you're smashed.

100
00:06:12,072 --> 00:06:14,564
I wasn't smashed.

101
00:06:15,542 --> 00:06:17,238
Well...

102
00:06:31,424 --> 00:06:36,328
Can I speak to Mr. Ross
Buckley, the editor? Ben Matlock.

103
00:06:37,197 --> 00:06:40,224
Have a seat. I'll let
him know you're here.

104
00:06:51,945 --> 00:06:54,312
Mr. Buckley a friend of yours?

105
00:06:54,481 --> 00:06:55,915
Oh, no, no.

106
00:06:56,549 --> 00:06:58,450
Mine, neither.

107
00:07:02,188 --> 00:07:03,781
Remember that story
they did on the man

108
00:07:03,957 --> 00:07:07,519
who was having a love affair
with an alien from another planet?

109
00:07:07,694 --> 00:07:09,526
No, I must have missed that one.

110
00:07:09,696 --> 00:07:10,925
That was me.

111
00:07:12,732 --> 00:07:16,066
How do these people think
they can make up stuff like that

112
00:07:16,236 --> 00:07:18,467
and get away with it?

113
00:07:18,638 --> 00:07:20,334
They didn't make it up.

114
00:07:20,507 --> 00:07:23,033
I am sexually
involved with an alien.

115
00:07:24,077 --> 00:07:27,878
Yes, and I was quite happy to
share my story with their reporter.

116
00:07:28,048 --> 00:07:32,713
But I wasn't happy when I saw it
in print. They made fun of me, of us.

117
00:07:36,189 --> 00:07:37,555
I'm gonna sue.

118
00:07:39,993 --> 00:07:42,553
Could you just get me in now?

119
00:07:50,303 --> 00:07:51,532
How about it?

120
00:07:52,939 --> 00:07:54,373
A retraction?

121
00:07:54,541 --> 00:07:57,909
Why on earth would
we print a retraction?

122
00:07:58,111 --> 00:08:01,013
You're implying my client
was drunk. She wasn't.

123
00:08:01,181 --> 00:08:03,514
She slipped and fell as she
was about to make a toast.

124
00:08:03,683 --> 00:08:05,151
The wine steward told me

125
00:08:05,318 --> 00:08:08,379
that three bottles of chardonnay
were brought to her table that day,

126
00:08:08,555 --> 00:08:10,956
and at least two other restaurant
employees saw her drinking.

127
00:08:11,124 --> 00:08:13,923
The wine was for her friends.
She had a couple of sips.

128
00:08:14,094 --> 00:08:15,995
Not according to this picture.

129
00:08:16,162 --> 00:08:18,495
You're misrepresenting
the facts and you know it.

130
00:08:18,665 --> 00:08:20,031
Mr. Matlock,

131
00:08:20,200 --> 00:08:23,364
I realize the $6 million
judgment we got hit with last week

132
00:08:23,536 --> 00:08:29,134
may make us seem like easy
prey, but I assure you, it was a fluke.

133
00:08:29,309 --> 00:08:32,438
We haven't lost a
case in, what, 12 years?

134
00:08:32,612 --> 00:08:37,107
Now, if your client wants to
sue, well, that's her prerogative.

135
00:08:37,283 --> 00:08:39,343
But you and I both know

136
00:08:39,953 --> 00:08:42,821
she hasn't got a
leg to stand on. Heh.

137
00:08:42,989 --> 00:08:44,981
No pun intended.

138
00:08:48,695 --> 00:08:51,255
Okay, then we sue.

139
00:08:51,431 --> 00:08:55,061
Well, Carol, suing's one
thing and winning's another.

140
00:08:55,235 --> 00:08:59,070
I was not drunk when that picture
was taken, and that's the truth.

141
00:08:59,239 --> 00:09:01,159
Isn't that the whole
purpose of our legal system?

142
00:09:01,307 --> 00:09:04,004
- To discern the truth?
- Well, yeah.

143
00:09:04,177 --> 00:09:06,612
Sue, then. What
are you afraid of?

144
00:09:06,780 --> 00:09:10,342
Well, the problem is, the burden
of proof doesn't lie with them.

145
00:09:10,517 --> 00:09:11,712
It lies with us.

146
00:09:11,885 --> 00:09:14,912
We would have to prove
beyond a shadow of doubt

147
00:09:15,088 --> 00:09:17,887
that you were stone-cold sober
that day, and we can't do that.

148
00:09:19,692 --> 00:09:22,161
So I'm just supposed to
roll over and play dead?

149
00:09:23,630 --> 00:09:25,929
- Well, it's a...
- Hand me that phone before you go,

150
00:09:26,099 --> 00:09:27,931
- would you, please?
- Yeah.

151
00:09:30,070 --> 00:09:32,471
- Thanks, anyway, Ben.
- Hm.

152
00:09:39,279 --> 00:09:41,874
The number for the
<i>National Informer,</i> please.

153
00:09:42,048 --> 00:09:44,540
I think you should not only
pay for my pain and suffering,

154
00:09:44,717 --> 00:09:46,549
but Leetauk's as well.

155
00:09:47,287 --> 00:09:48,550
Who's Leetauk?

156
00:09:48,721 --> 00:09:50,189
His alien lover.

157
00:09:51,791 --> 00:09:56,126
Ahem. Mr. Parsons, I really think
we're wasting each other's time here.

158
00:09:56,296 --> 00:09:58,731
- Leetauk is really upset.
- Oh.

159
00:09:58,898 --> 00:10:01,766
Well, you tell her that if
I ever get to her planet,

160
00:10:01,935 --> 00:10:03,198
I'll make it up to her, okay?

161
00:10:04,604 --> 00:10:07,367
<i>Mr. Buckley, Carol
Davis is on line one.</i>

162
00:10:07,540 --> 00:10:10,567
<i>- What do I tell her?</i>
- I'll take that.

163
00:10:10,743 --> 00:10:12,075
Hello?

164
00:10:12,245 --> 00:10:14,976
<i>Mr. Buckley, I'd like to
come in and talk to you today.</i>

165
00:10:15,148 --> 00:10:17,777
I'm afraid my schedule's
all filled up, Ms. Davis.

166
00:10:19,119 --> 00:10:21,145
I can come in after
your last appointment.

167
00:10:22,155 --> 00:10:24,590
All right, 7.

168
00:10:25,258 --> 00:10:27,090
Oh, I'll chill some wine.

169
00:10:27,260 --> 00:10:29,126
How about a nice chardonnay?

170
00:10:54,120 --> 00:10:56,021
Mr. Buckley?

171
00:10:59,159 --> 00:11:00,286
Mr. Buckley?

172
00:11:13,640 --> 00:11:15,268
Ross, I got these
proof sheets back.

173
00:11:15,441 --> 00:11:17,933
I'm not sure that
you're gonna...

174
00:11:55,615 --> 00:11:58,744
I just thought if I could talk to him, I
could appeal to his nobler instincts.

175
00:11:58,918 --> 00:12:00,045
You should have called me.

176
00:12:00,220 --> 00:12:02,189
What difference
would that have made?

177
00:12:02,355 --> 00:12:05,689
I could have told you he
didn't have any nobler instincts.

178
00:12:05,858 --> 00:12:08,828
I just talked to Lieutenant Sturtz.
They found the murder weapon.

179
00:12:08,995 --> 00:12:12,090
- What was it?
- It was this crystal trophy thing

180
00:12:12,265 --> 00:12:14,063
that Ross Buckley
had in his office.

181
00:12:14,234 --> 00:12:16,635
It was long and
sharp, like a dagger.

182
00:12:16,803 --> 00:12:19,068
They found it in your garage.

183
00:12:20,006 --> 00:12:23,374
Well, I didn't put it
there. I never even saw it.

184
00:12:23,543 --> 00:12:26,672
- Any prints?
- No, no, no, just blood.

185
00:12:28,982 --> 00:12:31,076
I'm being framed.

186
00:12:32,085 --> 00:12:36,420
Let's not forget, as fond
as we all were of Ross,

187
00:12:36,589 --> 00:12:39,320
his untimely death is news,

188
00:12:39,492 --> 00:12:42,394
and news was his
and is our business.

189
00:12:42,562 --> 00:12:45,361
John, I want a new
layout by 5 p.m. tomorrow.

190
00:12:45,531 --> 00:12:50,401
A photo that Nate got of the
body, front page, enlarged,

191
00:12:50,570 --> 00:12:53,699
so that it can be seen
easily from the checkout line.

192
00:12:55,074 --> 00:12:57,873
Excuse me, Ben Matlock,
Carol Davis' lawyer.

193
00:12:58,044 --> 00:13:00,275
This is Cliff Lewis.
Ms. Van Ness?

194
00:13:00,446 --> 00:13:02,142
Yes, and we're rather busy here.

195
00:13:02,315 --> 00:13:06,252
Oh, I expect, but since I
have you all here in one room,

196
00:13:06,419 --> 00:13:08,684
how about I question
everybody at once, save time?

197
00:13:08,855 --> 00:13:10,881
- Okay?
- Okay.

198
00:13:11,624 --> 00:13:17,689
Okay, now, who knew that Carol Davis
had an appointment with Mr. Buckley

199
00:13:17,864 --> 00:13:20,766
at 7:00 the night he died?

200
00:13:25,305 --> 00:13:29,299
Well, I know that
both of you knew.

201
00:13:29,475 --> 00:13:32,536
Mr. Buckley's secretary said
that you were with him in his office

202
00:13:32,712 --> 00:13:34,078
when he was
talking with that man,

203
00:13:34,247 --> 00:13:38,082
you know, who is having an "affair"
with that alien, when Carol called.

204
00:13:38,251 --> 00:13:39,981
Yes.

205
00:13:42,522 --> 00:13:45,287
Oh, I was there too.

206
00:13:45,458 --> 00:13:48,326
Uh, Chet Sellers,
photographer extraordinaire.

207
00:13:48,494 --> 00:13:49,826
Eh...

208
00:13:50,730 --> 00:13:53,461
"Iowa housewife gives
birth to 50-pound baby"?

209
00:13:53,633 --> 00:13:55,602
- What?
- It says so right here.

210
00:13:55,768 --> 00:13:57,498
Is that all, Mr. Matlock?

211
00:13:58,771 --> 00:14:02,970
Oh. Uh, did Mr. Buckley
have any enemies?

212
00:14:03,142 --> 00:14:06,635
Of course. He ran a newspaper.
He had hundreds of enemies.

213
00:14:06,813 --> 00:14:08,839
No, I mean around here.

214
00:14:09,682 --> 00:14:12,083
We all got along famously.

215
00:14:12,251 --> 00:14:13,810
- Sure.
- Absolutely.

216
00:14:14,887 --> 00:14:17,254
How could a woman this
small have a baby that big?

217
00:14:17,423 --> 00:14:18,823
- Let me see.
- Look.

218
00:14:19,926 --> 00:14:21,588
Goodbye, Mr. Matlock.

219
00:14:21,761 --> 00:14:23,787
Oh, yeah, goodbye.

220
00:14:23,963 --> 00:14:25,829
Goodbye.

221
00:14:26,866 --> 00:14:29,335
Can you just imagine the doctor?
"Congratulations, Mrs. Jones.

222
00:14:29,502 --> 00:14:32,529
Beautiful baby boy,
50 pounds, 3 feet tall.

223
00:14:32,705 --> 00:14:34,640
He'll be driving you
home from the hospital."

224
00:14:39,445 --> 00:14:44,281
Oh, out of curiosity, who'll
be the next editor-in-chief?

225
00:14:45,885 --> 00:14:47,183
I am.

226
00:14:47,353 --> 00:14:48,651
Oh.

227
00:14:54,360 --> 00:14:55,692
So you want me to follow him?

228
00:14:55,862 --> 00:14:57,490
Oh, yeah, everywhere.

229
00:14:57,663 --> 00:14:59,632
Find out where he goes,
who he hangs out with.

230
00:14:59,799 --> 00:15:01,893
I think our readers
will be real interested.

231
00:15:02,068 --> 00:15:03,908
So get lots of nice,
clear pictures, all right?

232
00:15:04,070 --> 00:15:06,369
No problem, boss.

233
00:15:07,340 --> 00:15:09,809
- Hi, John Nash?
- No, right there.

234
00:15:09,976 --> 00:15:11,496
Oh, hi. I'm Jerri
Stone. I'm a writer.

235
00:15:11,644 --> 00:15:13,844
I'm looking for a job. Your
job. Actually, your old job.

236
00:15:13,980 --> 00:15:16,100
Well, things are kind of
hectic around here right now,

237
00:15:16,249 --> 00:15:18,912
- I'll have to get back to you on these.
- Oh, I don't know.

238
00:15:19,085 --> 00:15:22,055
There's a piece in there on Sergio
Franklin, the baseball player's ex-wife.

239
00:15:22,221 --> 00:15:24,190
The real reason he divorced
her is she sleepwalks.

240
00:15:24,357 --> 00:15:26,451
Whenever she does, she
dresses up in men's clothing,

241
00:15:26,626 --> 00:15:29,425
speaks with an accent, and
says she's Henry Kissinger.

242
00:15:30,363 --> 00:15:32,423
It's true.

243
00:15:33,666 --> 00:15:34,998
What else you got?

244
00:15:35,168 --> 00:15:37,899
Uh, that's a piece on the chairman
of the board of McEvoy Industries.

245
00:15:38,070 --> 00:15:39,868
He likes to play with dolls.

246
00:15:40,339 --> 00:15:41,637
- You're kidding.
- No.

247
00:15:41,808 --> 00:15:45,267
All confirmed, ready to go to press.
You give me a job, they're yours.

248
00:15:47,613 --> 00:15:50,447
Here you go, pages one through
16. Rest should be ready in an hour.

249
00:15:50,616 --> 00:15:52,608
Linda Kirsch, meet
our newest reporter.

250
00:15:52,785 --> 00:15:53,878
- Jerri...?
- Stone.

251
00:15:54,053 --> 00:15:57,046
- Hi. What do you do?
- I'm an assistant editor.

252
00:15:57,223 --> 00:15:59,715
Oh, that's what I'm gonna
do. Well, eventually. Ha, ha, ha.

253
00:16:01,227 --> 00:16:03,492
All right, hotshot.

254
00:16:04,330 --> 00:16:06,458
First assignment.

255
00:16:07,300 --> 00:16:09,963
I want you to track down
the bum who fabricated this.

256
00:16:10,136 --> 00:16:12,571
We paid good money to
a guy named Williamson.

257
00:16:12,738 --> 00:16:14,738
His name turned out to
be as phony as that picture.

258
00:16:14,907 --> 00:16:16,068
You got it. Can I...?

259
00:16:16,242 --> 00:16:19,076
Get the guy who cost
us 6 million bucks, okay?

260
00:16:19,245 --> 00:16:21,214
All right, Linda, just
give her the nickel tour.

261
00:16:21,380 --> 00:16:23,144
Take her through
the basics, okay?

262
00:16:23,316 --> 00:16:26,377
Sure thing, boss. Come on.

263
00:16:28,521 --> 00:16:32,219
Police have Ross' office
cordoned off. We're short of space.

264
00:16:32,391 --> 00:16:35,623
- You'll be without an office for a while.
- Oh, that's okay.

265
00:16:35,795 --> 00:16:37,957
So, what was Ross like?

266
00:16:38,130 --> 00:16:41,567
Well, he hired me.
He had good judgment.

267
00:16:41,734 --> 00:16:45,637
He taught me everything
I know. He was brilliant.

268
00:16:45,805 --> 00:16:49,799
He hit on me every once in
a while. He had good taste.

269
00:16:55,648 --> 00:16:57,241
That's better. Right in there.

270
00:16:57,416 --> 00:17:01,444
You know, what I really need is
more red. I need rosier cheeks.

271
00:17:01,621 --> 00:17:04,557
- Oh, okay.
- They don't seem to come out there.

272
00:17:09,295 --> 00:17:12,629
Reverend Masters, I'm Jerri
Stone with the <i>National Informer.</i>

273
00:17:12,798 --> 00:17:15,632
And I'm trying to find out
who counterfeited this picture.

274
00:17:15,801 --> 00:17:17,599
Heh, heh. Why, you people did.

275
00:17:17,770 --> 00:17:20,171
Actually, the <i>National Informer</i>
thought it was the real McCoy

276
00:17:20,339 --> 00:17:22,001
until Heather Hartley
took the stand.

277
00:17:22,174 --> 00:17:24,507
I told them it was a
fake. They wouldn't listen.

278
00:17:24,677 --> 00:17:27,442
And the good Lord
nailed them for it.

279
00:17:28,548 --> 00:17:31,677
This was made by inserting a
picture of Heather into a photo

280
00:17:31,851 --> 00:17:34,514
taken of you outside the hotel.
So my next question would be...

281
00:17:34,687 --> 00:17:36,280
Do I remember
anyone taking my picture

282
00:17:36,455 --> 00:17:39,892
outside the Crown Regent
Hotel last month? Yes, I do.

283
00:17:40,059 --> 00:17:42,392
- You do?
- I know all four Gospels,

284
00:17:42,562 --> 00:17:46,431
the Book of Psalms, and the Book
of Revelation by heart, young lady.

285
00:17:46,599 --> 00:17:48,932
I have nothing if
not a good memory.

286
00:17:49,101 --> 00:17:54,904
The photographer was plainly dressed,
balding, rather short, and overweight.

287
00:17:56,509 --> 00:17:59,172
Why didn't you say
something before?

288
00:17:59,545 --> 00:18:01,776
None of you asked.

289
00:18:09,722 --> 00:18:12,191
- Here he comes.
- Hey, hey.

290
00:18:12,358 --> 00:18:14,589
- Look over here. Smile.
- Hey, sir.

291
00:18:14,760 --> 00:18:16,956
Come on, give us a smile.

292
00:18:25,905 --> 00:18:29,398
- Come on, one more.
- Hey.

293
00:18:41,887 --> 00:18:44,447
Ahem. Excuse me.

294
00:18:45,024 --> 00:18:47,357
I'm looking for a guy who
likes to take pictures of people.

295
00:18:48,728 --> 00:18:50,560
You know, without their consent.

296
00:18:51,197 --> 00:18:53,257
He's short, fat, and going bald.

297
00:18:53,432 --> 00:18:56,527
Sometimes goes by
the name of Williamson.

298
00:18:56,936 --> 00:19:00,031
- Can't help you.
- No.

299
00:19:00,840 --> 00:19:02,035
Never heard of him.

300
00:19:03,743 --> 00:19:05,769
So where else do
you guys hang out?

301
00:19:05,945 --> 00:19:08,414
- All over.
- Hey, there's Tony.

302
00:19:08,581 --> 00:19:11,983
- Hey, Tony, Tony, Tony!
- Tony, Tony!

303
00:19:16,188 --> 00:19:19,283
I've, uh, got what you need.

304
00:19:20,493 --> 00:19:21,552
You do?

305
00:19:22,328 --> 00:19:28,325
Yeah, how about a nice 8-by-10
of Donny Osmond in a Speedo?

306
00:19:29,735 --> 00:19:31,601
No, thanks.

307
00:19:54,427 --> 00:19:55,827
Hi, I'm Ben Matlock.

308
00:19:55,995 --> 00:19:59,591
We sort of met outside Ross
Buckley's office the other day.

309
00:19:59,765 --> 00:20:01,063
Yes, I remember.

310
00:20:01,233 --> 00:20:04,635
I'm a lawyer, and I represent the
woman who's accused of murdering him.

311
00:20:04,804 --> 00:20:06,500
- Carol Davis.
- Right.

312
00:20:07,173 --> 00:20:08,813
- Could I...? Thanks.
- Oh, sure. Come in.

313
00:20:13,179 --> 00:20:16,843
- You live here alone?
- Yes.

314
00:20:17,016 --> 00:20:19,576
We don't believe
in cohabitation.

315
00:20:19,752 --> 00:20:21,812
Oh, you mean you and...?

316
00:20:23,489 --> 00:20:28,154
- Oh, sit down.
- Let me jump right to the point.

317
00:20:28,661 --> 00:20:32,826
I understand that you
were in Mr. Buckley's office

318
00:20:32,998 --> 00:20:36,162
at the time Carol called and
arranged to meet him that night.

319
00:20:36,335 --> 00:20:38,827
Yes, I was.

320
00:20:39,004 --> 00:20:41,496
I'll bet you anything
that she was framed.

321
00:20:42,908 --> 00:20:45,434
I mean, whoever
actually killed Mr. Buckley

322
00:20:45,611 --> 00:20:50,982
had to know that she was going
to his office at 7:00 that night.

323
00:20:52,184 --> 00:20:53,584
No, thank you.

324
00:20:53,753 --> 00:20:57,622
But where were you at that
time? Just as a matter of course.

325
00:20:57,790 --> 00:20:58,849
I understand.

326
00:20:59,024 --> 00:21:02,688
As it happens, I was having a
personal moment with my paramour.

327
00:21:03,462 --> 00:21:05,158
Oh, you mean...?

328
00:21:06,899 --> 00:21:09,266
Where were you sharing
this personal moment?

329
00:21:09,435 --> 00:21:11,063
In her spaceship.

330
00:21:11,237 --> 00:21:12,535
She beamed you up?

331
00:21:13,272 --> 00:21:15,707
Don't be silly. That's
only in the movies.

332
00:21:17,243 --> 00:21:19,007
They parked out front.

333
00:21:19,178 --> 00:21:22,580
- The spaceship was parked out front?
- Right.

334
00:21:24,283 --> 00:21:27,253
Well, Mr. Parsons, if
there was a spaceship

335
00:21:27,419 --> 00:21:30,184
parked out in front of your
house at 7:00 in the evening,

336
00:21:30,356 --> 00:21:33,224
don't you think your
neighbors would have seen it?

337
00:21:33,826 --> 00:21:36,261
Oh, they did. They
see it all the time.

338
00:21:37,029 --> 00:21:40,261
- How come they never said anything?
- The aliens make them forget.

339
00:21:41,133 --> 00:21:43,227
They let me remember.

340
00:21:43,402 --> 00:21:44,700
Oh.

341
00:21:45,805 --> 00:21:51,711
Mr. Parsons, do you think maybe
your friend is outside watching us now?

342
00:21:52,344 --> 00:21:55,007
Oh, no, she and the
others left hours ago.

343
00:21:55,181 --> 00:21:56,649
Well, somebody sure is.

344
00:22:13,098 --> 00:22:16,364
All right, cut it out.
I said, cut it out.

345
00:22:16,535 --> 00:22:17,901
Can you see the caption now?

346
00:22:18,070 --> 00:22:21,097
"Ben Matlock caught
in alien love triangle."

347
00:22:21,273 --> 00:22:24,801
Funny. Funny. Uh-oh.

348
00:22:25,878 --> 00:22:29,007
Hey, hey! Hey, that's private
property you're destroying there.

349
00:22:29,582 --> 00:22:31,574
No!

350
00:22:31,750 --> 00:22:33,946
You're trespassing,
so I guess we're even.

351
00:23:06,619 --> 00:23:12,024
If this Wade Parsons had a gripe
against him, maybe he's the killer.

352
00:23:12,458 --> 00:23:15,622
You have to have some grasp of
reality to frame somebody for murder,

353
00:23:15,794 --> 00:23:19,231
- and this guy's...
- Maybe his alien did it.

354
00:23:19,431 --> 00:23:20,956
Oh, hot dogs.

355
00:23:21,166 --> 00:23:23,658
- Where you been?
- Working. Thanks for the dirt, Carol.

356
00:23:23,836 --> 00:23:25,896
- I got the job like
that: - What job?

357
00:23:26,071 --> 00:23:27,972
At the <i>National Informer.</i>

358
00:23:28,707 --> 00:23:30,175
You went undercover?

359
00:23:30,943 --> 00:23:32,707
Yeah, why not? That's what I do.

360
00:23:32,912 --> 00:23:35,609
I was a cop. I've been at
drug busts. I've been shot at.

361
00:23:35,781 --> 00:23:38,683
Believe me, the <i>National
Informer's</i> not a dangerous place.

362
00:23:39,184 --> 00:23:40,550
Don't be so sure.

363
00:23:40,753 --> 00:23:43,052
I wanted to look around, but
they've got me on assignment.

364
00:23:43,255 --> 00:23:44,689
I wish you'd asked me.

365
00:23:44,890 --> 00:23:48,054
I don't like you doing stuff
like that without talking to me.

366
00:23:48,260 --> 00:23:50,923
- Why? Would you worry?
- Yeah.

367
00:23:53,098 --> 00:23:54,794
Cliff?

368
00:23:55,000 --> 00:23:57,435
- Cliff, what are you doing?
- Reading.

369
00:23:57,636 --> 00:23:59,571
You're reading the
<i>National Informer?</i>

370
00:23:59,738 --> 00:24:02,708
Not just looking at the trashy
pictures? Actually reading this garbage?

371
00:24:02,875 --> 00:24:05,777
- Hot dog?
- Yeah, a couple.

372
00:24:05,978 --> 00:24:07,469
Pig parts.

373
00:24:08,380 --> 00:24:09,712
Says here some
brands of hot dogs

374
00:24:09,915 --> 00:24:12,384
have as many as 40 different
types of pig parts in them.

375
00:24:14,053 --> 00:24:16,045
Says so right here.

376
00:24:24,129 --> 00:24:27,361
I had Neil set up a couple
sample pages in the new type style.

377
00:24:27,566 --> 00:24:29,501
- Good.
- What's going on?

378
00:24:30,202 --> 00:24:32,722
I'm just trying to give the
<i>Informer</i> a younger look, that's all.

379
00:24:32,905 --> 00:24:34,703
Try to pull in some
of that MTV crowd.

380
00:24:35,074 --> 00:24:38,010
Mrs. Van Ness can't
stand the MTV crowd.

381
00:24:38,577 --> 00:24:40,569
Well, you know, she can now.

382
00:24:40,779 --> 00:24:43,874
She and I have had a
lot of nice long talks lately.

383
00:24:44,583 --> 00:24:46,643
I'll bet you have.

384
00:24:46,852 --> 00:24:48,218
What's your problem, Linda?

385
00:24:49,321 --> 00:24:52,291
What the hell do you
think my problem is?

386
00:24:52,491 --> 00:24:54,483
This job was
supposed to be mine.

387
00:24:54,994 --> 00:24:56,587
Mrs. Van Ness promised.

388
00:24:56,795 --> 00:24:59,822
Well, I'm sorry. I guess
she changed her mind.

389
00:25:00,299 --> 00:25:02,734
You changed her mind.

390
00:25:02,935 --> 00:25:04,403
What did you tell her anyway?

391
00:25:04,603 --> 00:25:06,367
That Ross and I were
sleeping together?

392
00:25:06,872 --> 00:25:08,340
Of course not.

393
00:25:08,507 --> 00:25:11,341
- Liar.
- Linda, settle down.

394
00:25:11,844 --> 00:25:13,779
At least you've still got a job.

395
00:25:14,413 --> 00:25:17,247
What goes around
comes around, John boy.

396
00:25:17,416 --> 00:25:18,941
Just remember that.

397
00:25:23,455 --> 00:25:27,119
Oh. Excuse me. I'm
sorry. Is this a bad time?

398
00:25:27,326 --> 00:25:29,921
I was just wondering
about my car mileage.

399
00:25:30,095 --> 00:25:31,735
Am I supposed to be
keeping track of that?

400
00:25:31,864 --> 00:25:33,560
You got anything on
that photograph yet?

401
00:25:34,199 --> 00:25:36,065
- Nothing solid, but I...
- Well, get on it.

402
00:25:36,568 --> 00:25:39,436
We'll sweat the
small stuff later.

403
00:25:40,005 --> 00:25:41,564
Right.

404
00:25:44,877 --> 00:25:46,277
Bye.

405
00:25:57,656 --> 00:25:59,716
Hello, Mr. Matlock.

406
00:25:59,925 --> 00:26:01,791
Uh, Mr. Parsons.

407
00:26:01,994 --> 00:26:04,896
- Won't you come in?
- Thank you.

408
00:26:06,498 --> 00:26:10,128
This is my associate,
Cliff Lewis. Wade Parsons.

409
00:26:10,335 --> 00:26:13,635
- Oh, nice to meet you.
- Nice to meet you.

410
00:26:13,839 --> 00:26:16,536
I have some very exciting
news for you, Mr. Matlock.

411
00:26:16,742 --> 00:26:19,234
I would have called, but the
authorities have tapped my phone.

412
00:26:19,445 --> 00:26:21,311
You need to come
with me immediately.

413
00:26:21,480 --> 00:26:23,813
- What for?
- One of the aliens has a crush on you.

414
00:26:23,982 --> 00:26:25,280
She wants to meet you.

415
00:26:30,456 --> 00:26:33,449
Oh. That's very nice.

416
00:26:33,659 --> 00:26:36,099
Come on, Ben, the least you
could do is climb on the spaceship

417
00:26:36,261 --> 00:26:37,957
and have a glass
of Tang with the girl.

418
00:26:38,130 --> 00:26:40,292
How does she know about Ben?

419
00:26:40,466 --> 00:26:42,992
She overheard our
conversation this morning.

420
00:26:43,202 --> 00:26:44,602
They hear and see everything.

421
00:26:44,803 --> 00:26:49,400
And she was very impressed by
your mental acuity and physical grace.

422
00:26:49,608 --> 00:26:52,100
- His physical grace?
- Yes, my physical grace.

423
00:26:52,311 --> 00:26:54,439
Actually, she finds
you very attractive.

424
00:26:55,981 --> 00:26:57,540
Well, uh...

425
00:26:58,217 --> 00:27:01,710
Well, tell her I'm sorry.

426
00:27:01,920 --> 00:27:04,082
Maybe we can meet
in a couple of weeks.

427
00:27:04,289 --> 00:27:06,952
Come on, Ben. How often
do you get an offer like this?

428
00:27:07,159 --> 00:27:08,959
- Why don't you go?
- She's not wild about me.

429
00:27:09,161 --> 00:27:10,754
She's wild about you.

430
00:27:10,963 --> 00:27:12,625
Hey, Wade, what
does she look like?

431
00:27:12,831 --> 00:27:15,801
Well, like all of them, she
pretty much defies description.

432
00:27:16,568 --> 00:27:20,300
But I can tell you, she has
all the necessary attributes.

433
00:27:21,773 --> 00:27:24,470
- She does?
- In spades.

434
00:27:25,210 --> 00:27:27,736
Time's a-wasting, Mr. Matlock.

435
00:27:29,314 --> 00:27:34,480
Oh, no, no, no, I'm
too busy right now

436
00:27:34,653 --> 00:27:37,919
- to have a blind date with an alien.
- Ben.

437
00:27:41,827 --> 00:27:44,228
Help! Help!

438
00:27:46,832 --> 00:27:48,994
Down! Down!

439
00:27:49,168 --> 00:27:51,865
Aah! Down! Stop it!

440
00:27:52,037 --> 00:27:53,471
Go on! Get out of here!

441
00:27:53,672 --> 00:27:56,267
Down, boy, down!
What's so funny?

442
00:27:57,910 --> 00:28:00,402
Could you help me, please?

443
00:28:00,579 --> 00:28:01,945
Not my dog.

444
00:28:02,147 --> 00:28:04,912
He never bothers me because
I know all his commands.

445
00:28:05,684 --> 00:28:06,743
Oh, yeah?

446
00:28:06,952 --> 00:28:09,353
Well, could you please get
him off of me? Down, boy!

447
00:28:15,994 --> 00:28:18,259
No way. Down.

448
00:28:22,434 --> 00:28:24,426
Easy, boy.

449
00:28:25,604 --> 00:28:26,799
Go home, Poochie.

450
00:28:29,708 --> 00:28:31,506
You too.

451
00:28:31,710 --> 00:28:33,838
"Go home, Poochie"?

452
00:28:43,789 --> 00:28:44,882
Hi.

453
00:28:45,057 --> 00:28:46,548
- Afternoon.
- Is this you?

454
00:28:48,026 --> 00:28:51,588
- Yeah, my only claim to fame.
- Are you the guy that told the <i>Informer</i>

455
00:28:51,763 --> 00:28:53,789
that the Reverend Masters
was with a girl that day?

456
00:28:53,966 --> 00:28:56,765
No, I said he may have
been with a girl that day,

457
00:28:56,935 --> 00:29:00,497
that he comes here a lot with all
kinds of people, on business, you know.

458
00:29:00,706 --> 00:29:02,766
I guess they took
that to mean yes.

459
00:29:03,408 --> 00:29:06,276
Did you get a good look at the guy
who took the picture of the Reverend?

460
00:29:06,445 --> 00:29:08,346
I sure did. That's probably
why I can't remember

461
00:29:08,513 --> 00:29:11,312
whether Reverend Masters
was with a girl or not.

462
00:29:11,483 --> 00:29:13,163
I was too busy looking
at the photographer.

463
00:29:13,352 --> 00:29:16,550
- Short, fat, balding guy, right?
- No, he was average height.

464
00:29:16,755 --> 00:29:18,519
He had, like, bushy eyebrows,

465
00:29:18,724 --> 00:29:21,319
a weird haircut, and a
great handlebar mustache.

466
00:29:21,493 --> 00:29:24,429
That's why I was looking at him. You
just don't see many of those anymore.

467
00:29:25,897 --> 00:29:27,627
Thanks.

468
00:29:31,870 --> 00:29:34,101
One more, one more, one more!

469
00:29:34,273 --> 00:29:36,765
- Come on.
- Come on.

470
00:29:36,942 --> 00:29:41,312
- Big smile, big smile.
- Smile for us, come on.

471
00:29:46,885 --> 00:29:49,116
Hi, how you doing? Can
I talk to you for a sec?

472
00:29:49,321 --> 00:29:51,813
Sorry, gotta go.

473
00:29:52,658 --> 00:29:54,684
Hey, wait a minute!

474
00:30:09,141 --> 00:30:12,134
Hey, you know that guy that just
split? The one with the funny mustache?

475
00:30:12,344 --> 00:30:14,711
- Yeah.
- You know his name?

476
00:30:20,852 --> 00:30:22,218
Package for Leo Harmon.

477
00:30:26,658 --> 00:30:28,320
I lied.

478
00:30:29,361 --> 00:30:32,126
Well, would you
take a look at all this?

479
00:30:32,331 --> 00:30:34,323
Hey, get out of here.

480
00:30:37,436 --> 00:30:39,701
Oh, look, are you dummying
up yet another picture

481
00:30:39,871 --> 00:30:42,932
- on your electronic paint box?
- What do you want?

482
00:30:43,141 --> 00:30:45,576
Who paid you to forge that
picture of the Reverend Masters?

483
00:30:45,777 --> 00:30:47,507
I didn't forge any picture.

484
00:30:47,713 --> 00:30:51,275
You can talk to us, you can
talk to the cops. It's your call.

485
00:30:52,150 --> 00:30:53,750
What makes you think
I work for somebody?

486
00:30:53,919 --> 00:30:56,718
You sold that picture to
the <i>Informer</i> for peanuts.

487
00:30:56,888 --> 00:30:58,288
So either you're
incredibly stupid,

488
00:30:58,490 --> 00:31:01,460
or you knew you'd be getting some
real money from somebody else.

489
00:31:03,528 --> 00:31:05,429
It was the Reverend
Masters, wasn't it?

490
00:31:05,597 --> 00:31:08,726
That's why he tried to throw me off
with that "short, fat, bald guy" routine.

491
00:31:08,900 --> 00:31:10,630
He didn't want me
to find you, did he?

492
00:31:11,536 --> 00:31:13,368
Maybe it was the
Reverend, maybe it wasn't.

493
00:31:14,206 --> 00:31:17,108
Well, a simple check of your bank
records and his ought to confirm that.

494
00:31:20,178 --> 00:31:21,669
Of course there is a possibility

495
00:31:21,880 --> 00:31:24,200
that Reverend Masters figured
out a way to cover his tracks

496
00:31:24,383 --> 00:31:26,183
and then leave this guy
holding the bag, yeah.

497
00:31:26,385 --> 00:31:28,980
Just in case someone
like us ever caught on.

498
00:31:29,154 --> 00:31:30,645
Hmm...

499
00:31:34,326 --> 00:31:37,819
- It wasn't just us.
- No?

500
00:31:39,064 --> 00:31:43,092
Look, Ross Buckley and the
Reverend were in on it together.

501
00:31:43,769 --> 00:31:46,129
Buckley made sure the <i>Informer</i>
printed the picture I rigged,

502
00:31:46,304 --> 00:31:48,398
Masters sued, they cut
me a chunk of the 6 mill

503
00:31:48,573 --> 00:31:50,337
and they split the
rest down the middle.

504
00:31:51,910 --> 00:31:54,004
Ross Buckley
defrauded his own paper?

505
00:31:54,479 --> 00:31:56,448
Somebody didn't like that.

506
00:32:03,588 --> 00:32:07,047
<i>I will miss your bright eyes</i>

507
00:32:07,225 --> 00:32:09,057
<i>And sweet smile</i>

508
00:32:09,227 --> 00:32:15,167
<i>For they say you are
taking The sunshine</i>

509
00:32:15,367 --> 00:32:20,965
<i>That has brightened
our pathway A while</i>

510
00:32:21,173 --> 00:32:26,077
<i>Come and sit by my
side If you love me</i>

511
00:32:26,745 --> 00:32:31,945
<i>Do not hasten to bid me adieu</i>

512
00:32:32,150 --> 00:32:37,418
<i>But remember the
Red River Valley</i>

513
00:32:37,589 --> 00:32:42,755
<i>And the cowboy
Who loves you so true</i>

514
00:32:45,096 --> 00:32:48,828
I can't believe Ross Buckley
sold out his entire paper.

515
00:32:49,000 --> 00:32:51,663
Just money. Of course, I
wouldn't give you three cents

516
00:32:51,837 --> 00:32:54,864
for the whole kit and caboodle,
<i>National Informer</i> and all of them.

517
00:32:55,073 --> 00:32:56,803
I don't believe a
word they print.

518
00:32:57,008 --> 00:32:59,705
- You like pimento in your bologna?
- It's okay.

519
00:32:59,878 --> 00:33:03,144
Not me. I like bologna
to taste like bologna.

520
00:33:03,348 --> 00:33:06,079
You know what I like
summertime? Tomato sandwiches.

521
00:33:06,251 --> 00:33:08,516
Just bread, mayonnaise,
and sliced tomato.

522
00:33:08,687 --> 00:33:09,985
Mm-hm.

523
00:33:10,222 --> 00:33:15,092
<i>Come and sit by my
side If you love me</i>

524
00:33:15,293 --> 00:33:18,786
<i>Do not hasten to bid me adieu</i>

525
00:33:18,964 --> 00:33:20,444
You sure like singing
those old songs.

526
00:33:20,599 --> 00:33:21,623
<i>But remember...</i>

527
00:33:21,800 --> 00:33:24,133
- Oh, yes, yes, I love the old songs.
- Me too.

528
00:33:24,302 --> 00:33:26,703
They relax me, ease my mind.

529
00:33:27,939 --> 00:33:30,374
- You need that in this business, yeah.
- Yeah.

530
00:33:30,575 --> 00:33:32,703
Of course sometimes
when I'm working on a case,

531
00:33:32,911 --> 00:33:35,437
and I'm, you know,
something comes to me.

532
00:33:36,014 --> 00:33:37,312
That happens to me too.

533
00:33:37,482 --> 00:33:38,506
- Yeah?
- Yeah.

534
00:33:38,717 --> 00:33:40,777
Well, work and relax
at the same time.

535
00:33:42,988 --> 00:33:48,990
<i>Down in the valley Valley so low</i>

536
00:33:49,561 --> 00:33:56,264
<i>Hang your head over
Hear the wind blow</i>

537
00:33:56,468 --> 00:34:02,768
<i>Hear the wind blow,
love Hear the wind blow</i>

538
00:34:03,275 --> 00:34:10,807
<i>Hang your head over
Hear the wind blow</i>

539
00:34:12,984 --> 00:34:14,350
Good.

540
00:34:15,353 --> 00:34:19,449
You know, Carol
Davis didn't kill anybody.

541
00:34:19,658 --> 00:34:23,618
Of course I don't think she's
gonna run for the Senate either.

542
00:34:23,828 --> 00:34:26,354
If she does, I'm not
gonna vote for her.

543
00:34:26,898 --> 00:34:30,096
- No?
- Scatterbrain.

544
00:34:30,268 --> 00:34:31,759
Yeah, she is.

545
00:34:31,970 --> 00:34:34,963
- Jumping from one thing to the next.
- Yeah.

546
00:34:37,742 --> 00:34:39,142
So, what are you gonna do now?

547
00:34:40,011 --> 00:34:42,171
After I finish picking this
pimento out of the bologna,

548
00:34:42,347 --> 00:34:43,781
I'm gonna eat this sandwich.

549
00:34:44,516 --> 00:34:48,544
Well, maybe I'll go
and see if I can find Cliff.

550
00:34:53,858 --> 00:34:56,487
Why are you walking like that?

551
00:34:56,661 --> 00:34:57,685
Oh.

552
00:34:57,862 --> 00:35:02,323
Well, you know this Wade Parsons
that they wrote about in the <i>Informer?</i>

553
00:35:02,500 --> 00:35:04,162
Guy with the alien girlfriend?

554
00:35:04,369 --> 00:35:09,307
Well, he said that his
girlfriend has a girlfriend

555
00:35:09,507 --> 00:35:13,877
who said she thinks I have
a certain physical grace.

556
00:35:14,045 --> 00:35:16,708
And I was just checking
to see if I had any.

557
00:35:18,316 --> 00:35:21,753
Well, I think you have a very nice
physical grace for a man your age.

558
00:35:24,122 --> 00:35:25,681
Thank you.

559
00:35:43,475 --> 00:35:47,810
Old Ross Buckley was in cahoots
with the good Reverend Masters, huh?

560
00:35:48,013 --> 00:35:50,141
Yeah, Jerri's trying to
track the money now.

561
00:35:50,348 --> 00:35:52,817
Well, I don't know
how it'll help us,

562
00:35:53,018 --> 00:35:57,319
unless we find out that
Reverend Masters has all $6 million

563
00:35:57,522 --> 00:36:00,185
and doesn't have a you-know
for the time of the murder.

564
00:36:00,392 --> 00:36:04,921
Well, he does have an
alibi. I've already checked.

565
00:36:08,600 --> 00:36:10,933
I wonder what this is.

566
00:36:11,569 --> 00:36:13,265
Hm.

567
00:36:13,905 --> 00:36:15,533
Look at that.

568
00:36:16,041 --> 00:36:17,134
What are you doing here?

569
00:36:17,342 --> 00:36:21,780
Oh, we were just in the neighborhood
and we thought we'd look around.

570
00:36:22,113 --> 00:36:23,843
Any idea what this is?

571
00:36:24,049 --> 00:36:25,608
No, I've never seen it before.

572
00:36:25,784 --> 00:36:27,912
There on the floor.

573
00:36:30,221 --> 00:36:33,219
No... Wait a minute.

574
00:36:33,425 --> 00:36:36,623
That's Ross' watch. I was
with him when he bought it.

575
00:36:36,828 --> 00:36:39,354
You mean the other person
in this picture's Ross Buckley?

576
00:36:39,564 --> 00:36:40,862
Yeah.

577
00:36:41,066 --> 00:36:44,002
What the hell would he be doing
having a drink with Wesley Masters?

578
00:36:44,202 --> 00:36:47,468
- Where was this taken?
- I don't know.

579
00:36:48,640 --> 00:36:50,199
Oh.

580
00:36:50,875 --> 00:36:53,344
But I have a damn
good idea who took it.

581
00:37:12,831 --> 00:37:15,096
Well, ha, ha, ha.

582
00:37:19,204 --> 00:37:20,866
A little gardening, huh?

583
00:37:21,072 --> 00:37:24,338
- Anything to keep my mind off of it.
- Yeah.

584
00:37:25,543 --> 00:37:26,806
Who's that?

585
00:37:27,011 --> 00:37:29,776
Photographer from
the <i>National Informer.</i>

586
00:37:29,981 --> 00:37:32,143
I don't want him taking
my picture again, Ben.

587
00:37:32,350 --> 00:37:34,910
He's not after you.
He's after me. Come on.

588
00:37:35,120 --> 00:37:37,589
- Well, where are we going?
- Little walk down the block.

589
00:37:37,789 --> 00:37:40,418
Take five minutes. There you go.

590
00:38:16,194 --> 00:38:21,826
Now, I understand that you're a
high-school dropout, Mr. Sellers.

591
00:38:22,033 --> 00:38:24,093
- That's right.
- Why'd you drop out?

592
00:38:25,036 --> 00:38:26,971
Well, I wanted to
be a photographer

593
00:38:27,172 --> 00:38:29,698
- and high school was a waste of time.
- Yeah.

594
00:38:30,708 --> 00:38:34,270
But didn't your lack of education
and experience get in your way

595
00:38:34,479 --> 00:38:37,278
- when you tried to find a job?
- For a while.

596
00:38:37,482 --> 00:38:38,916
If the truth be told,

597
00:38:39,117 --> 00:38:41,848
you were turned down by
every paper you applied to

598
00:38:42,053 --> 00:38:44,045
except the <i>National
Informer,</i> weren't you?

599
00:38:45,757 --> 00:38:48,852
- Well, that's right, yeah.
- Why'd the <i>Informer</i> want you?

600
00:38:49,060 --> 00:38:52,053
Because they knew I was
good, and Ross could see that.

601
00:38:53,231 --> 00:38:56,326
- Ross Buckley hired you?
- Yeah, that's right.

602
00:38:56,534 --> 00:38:58,696
Well, he must have
really gone to bat for you.

603
00:38:58,903 --> 00:39:01,566
- Yeah, he went to bat for me, all right.
- Yeah.

604
00:39:01,739 --> 00:39:03,674
And you were loyal
to him, weren't you?

605
00:39:03,875 --> 00:39:05,503
- You bet.
- Yeah.

606
00:39:05,710 --> 00:39:08,077
You were so loyal
that earlier this year,

607
00:39:08,279 --> 00:39:10,976
when another tabloid
tried to hire you away,

608
00:39:11,182 --> 00:39:13,913
you turned them down even though
they were gonna double your pay.

609
00:39:14,419 --> 00:39:15,648
- That's right.
- Yeah.

610
00:39:15,854 --> 00:39:18,414
And then your Mr. Buckley

611
00:39:18,590 --> 00:39:21,219
cost your paper $6 million
because of that lawsuit.

612
00:39:21,426 --> 00:39:23,122
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

613
00:39:23,328 --> 00:39:24,660
What'd you do about it?

614
00:39:26,064 --> 00:39:28,465
- What do you mean?
- Well, I know firsthand

615
00:39:28,666 --> 00:39:31,727
how good you are at following
people around with a camera.

616
00:39:31,936 --> 00:39:35,805
So maybe you started following
Reverend Masters around.

617
00:39:35,974 --> 00:39:38,910
You know, a little revenge, catch
him with something that really hurt him.

618
00:39:39,077 --> 00:39:40,477
No, I didn't do any of that.

619
00:39:40,645 --> 00:39:44,104
Uh, you didn't
sneak out one night

620
00:39:45,750 --> 00:39:47,776
and take this picture of him

621
00:39:47,986 --> 00:39:50,581
having a drink with
somebody in his office?

622
00:39:50,755 --> 00:39:52,280
No.

623
00:39:52,490 --> 00:39:54,755
But both the new
editor-in-chief of the <i>Informer</i>

624
00:39:54,959 --> 00:39:58,020
and his photo editor say
that this blemish right here...

625
00:39:58,229 --> 00:40:01,529
See, Your Honor?
Blemish right there.

626
00:40:02,300 --> 00:40:05,168
They both say that
every picture you take

627
00:40:05,370 --> 00:40:08,431
with your long
200-millimeter lens

628
00:40:08,640 --> 00:40:13,635
has this mark in the
lower left-hand quadrant.

629
00:40:18,650 --> 00:40:20,243
Your lens is scratched.

630
00:40:20,451 --> 00:40:22,784
So you must have taken it.

631
00:40:23,121 --> 00:40:25,882
Somebody must've borrowed my
lens, because I didn't take that picture.

632
00:40:26,024 --> 00:40:27,788
Yes, you did, and I know why.

633
00:40:27,992 --> 00:40:33,363
Because the man having a drink
with Reverend Masters is Ross Buckley.

634
00:40:34,666 --> 00:40:36,635
And that's when you first knew

635
00:40:36,834 --> 00:40:42,637
that the two of them were sharing
$6 million and you got none of it.

636
00:40:42,941 --> 00:40:43,965
No.

637
00:40:45,476 --> 00:40:48,310
People who work for the <i>Informer</i>

638
00:40:48,713 --> 00:40:52,445
have a way of taking two different
pictures and putting them together

639
00:40:52,650 --> 00:40:55,620
to make it look like something's
happening when nothing is happening.

640
00:40:55,820 --> 00:40:57,345
- Isn't that right?
- What do you mean?

641
00:40:57,555 --> 00:41:00,525
Well, like that picture of
the Reverend and the girl.

642
00:41:00,725 --> 00:41:02,887
That was a fake. That was
two pictures put together.

643
00:41:03,094 --> 00:41:05,461
- Well, I didn't do that.
- I know.

644
00:41:05,663 --> 00:41:07,655
That's my point.

645
00:41:07,865 --> 00:41:11,358
You ever heard the expression
"honor among thieves"?

646
00:41:11,569 --> 00:41:13,435
There is none.

647
00:41:13,871 --> 00:41:16,568
You didn't take that one,

648
00:41:17,742 --> 00:41:21,543
because you were passed over.

649
00:41:21,746 --> 00:41:24,841
Mr. Buckley gave that
job to somebody else.

650
00:41:25,049 --> 00:41:28,383
Maybe he thought you weren't
good enough or smart enough.

651
00:41:28,553 --> 00:41:31,921
Six million dollars for
just a little bit of work.

652
00:41:32,123 --> 00:41:35,389
And you didn't get any of
it. It went to another guy.

653
00:41:35,560 --> 00:41:37,620
No loyalty to you.

654
00:41:38,796 --> 00:41:40,458
So you printed up this picture

655
00:41:40,631 --> 00:41:42,657
and you confronted
Mr. Buckley with it, didn't you?

656
00:41:42,867 --> 00:41:45,166
- No, I didn't do that.
- I'm sure that he denied

657
00:41:45,370 --> 00:41:47,862
that he and the good Reverend
were co-conspirators in a plot

658
00:41:48,072 --> 00:41:51,372
to defraud the <i>Informer</i> of $6 million,
but you knew he was lying, didn't you?

659
00:41:51,576 --> 00:41:54,478
- No, I didn't, all right?
- No honor among thieves.

660
00:41:54,679 --> 00:41:57,774
The man to whom you had been
so loyal in destroying other people

661
00:41:57,982 --> 00:42:00,474
didn't have enough faith
in you to give you the job

662
00:42:00,651 --> 00:42:03,644
of stealing a little money from
the paper you both worked for.

663
00:42:04,389 --> 00:42:07,587
He was just a scheming,
common criminal.

664
00:42:07,792 --> 00:42:09,954
And unfair to you.

665
00:42:10,495 --> 00:42:14,694
You must have felt
betrayed. Betrayed and mad.

666
00:42:14,899 --> 00:42:18,233
Madder than hell. So
mad you tore up that picture

667
00:42:18,436 --> 00:42:20,803
and grabbed that
crystal spike off the shelf

668
00:42:20,972 --> 00:42:23,441
- and stabbed him to death.
- No, I didn't do any of that.

669
00:42:23,608 --> 00:42:25,008
Then panic set in.

670
00:42:25,209 --> 00:42:28,702
You picked up as many pieces
of the picture as you could find

671
00:42:28,913 --> 00:42:30,313
and ran to your car.

672
00:42:30,515 --> 00:42:33,314
And then you
remembered Carol Davis.

673
00:42:33,518 --> 00:42:35,510
You drove your car to her house

674
00:42:35,720 --> 00:42:38,383
and planted the murder
weapon in her garage, didn't you?

675
00:42:38,589 --> 00:42:39,852
No, I didn't do that.

676
00:42:42,326 --> 00:42:45,592
You didn't drive your
car to Carol Davis' house

677
00:42:45,797 --> 00:42:48,266
- the night of the murder?
- No.

678
00:42:48,800 --> 00:42:51,531
- You ever been to Carol Davis' house?
- No.

679
00:42:54,672 --> 00:42:58,803
Okay, once, sort of. I parked across
the street from her house yesterday.

680
00:42:59,210 --> 00:43:00,701
So you could watch Carol and me

681
00:43:00,912 --> 00:43:02,471
- walk down the block?
- That's right.

682
00:43:02,680 --> 00:43:05,013
Well, the reason she and
I walked down the block

683
00:43:05,216 --> 00:43:09,483
is so her neighbors could get
a look at you and your little car.

684
00:43:09,654 --> 00:43:12,920
See, I had prearranged so
that they could be peeping

685
00:43:13,091 --> 00:43:17,495
out of their windows
when you passed by.

686
00:43:17,962 --> 00:43:20,124
So once again,

687
00:43:20,765 --> 00:43:23,963
were you at Carol Davis'
house the night of the murder?

688
00:43:24,936 --> 00:43:27,576
You might as well answer, because
as soon as you get off the stand,

689
00:43:27,772 --> 00:43:29,240
I'm gonna call one
of her neighbors.

690
00:43:29,440 --> 00:43:32,968
And he's gonna
say yes, he saw you

691
00:43:33,444 --> 00:43:36,312
and your van at her house

692
00:43:36,514 --> 00:43:40,315
a little after 7 the
night of the murder.

693
00:43:40,551 --> 00:43:46,650
He even wrote "wash me" in
the dust of your back window.

694
00:43:47,992 --> 00:43:52,896
You were there trying to frame my
client for a crime you had committed.

695
00:43:54,665 --> 00:43:57,032
No loyalty among thieves.

696
00:43:57,235 --> 00:44:01,070
Mr. Sellers, Mr. Buckley,
the good reverend.

697
00:44:01,572 --> 00:44:04,303
No loyalty among thieves.

698
00:44:21,826 --> 00:44:23,988
Mr. Parsons?

699
00:44:27,031 --> 00:44:29,159
Mr. Parsons?

700
00:44:36,874 --> 00:44:39,969
- Oh, Mr. Matlock, hello.
- Hello.

701
00:44:40,178 --> 00:44:43,376
I just stopped by to say hello.

702
00:44:43,581 --> 00:44:45,140
Oh, hello.

703
00:44:45,349 --> 00:44:48,717
Oh, well, you know that big case
I was working on, the Davis case?

704
00:44:48,920 --> 00:44:52,254
Well, it's over and we won.

705
00:44:52,890 --> 00:44:55,917
- Congratulations.
- Thank you.

706
00:44:56,127 --> 00:44:59,620
And I thought that since I finally
have some time on my hands,

707
00:44:59,830 --> 00:45:02,629
it might be nice if we could,

708
00:45:02,833 --> 00:45:06,497
you know, kind of socialize.

709
00:45:08,206 --> 00:45:10,971
Oh, you're too
late. They just left.

710
00:45:11,175 --> 00:45:14,407
- They've gone?
- Yeah, she had to go back.

711
00:45:14,579 --> 00:45:16,946
I'll never see her again.

712
00:45:17,148 --> 00:45:20,016
I begged her to let me
remember her, and she did.

713
00:45:21,852 --> 00:45:23,548
But,

714
00:45:23,821 --> 00:45:30,125
now it hurts so much that I
wish she'd made me forget.

715
